{"name":"Rain Song","hex":"#c5d5e9","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Condensed water falling from a cloud.","Any matter moving or falling, usually through air, and especially if liquid or otherwise figuratively identifiable with raindrops."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To have rain fall from the sky.","To fall as or like rain."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rain"},{"term":"song","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A musical composition with lyrics for voice or voices, performed by singing.","Any musical composition."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/song"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rainmaking (ritual)","blocks":[{"text":"Rainmaking is a weather modification ritual that attempts to invoke rain. It is based on the belief that humans can influence nature, spirits, or their ancestors who withhold or bring rain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Harar_Dance.jpg/330px-Harar_Dance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainmaking_(ritual)"},{"title":"Rain Song (al-Sayyab)","blocks":[{"text":"Rain Song is a famous 1960 poetry collection and Arabic poem by Badr Shakir al-Sayyab One of the \"great poems in modern Arabic poetry\", it has been compared to T.S. Eliot's The Waste Land."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rain_Song_(al-Sayyab)"},{"title":"Rain","blocks":[{"text":"Rain or rainfall is a form of atmospheric precipitation composed of droplets of water aerosols that have condensed from atmospheric water vapor and fall by gravity. Depending on the intensity, rain can be described as a drizzle, a shower, a cloudburst or a downpour. It is also often accompanied by other weather phenomena such as strong wind (rainstorm) and lightning (thunderstorm), and may be mixed with hail/ice pellet, snow (sleet) and sandstorm. Rain is a major component of the water cycle and is responsible for depositing most of the fresh water on Earth. It provides water for hydroelectric power plants, crop irrigation, and suitable conditions for many types of ecosystems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Hard_rain_on_a_roof.jpg/330px-Hard_rain_on_a_roof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rain"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Harar_Dance.jpg/330px-Harar_Dance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Harar_Dance.jpg/330px-Harar_Dance.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A rain dance being performed in Harar, Eastern Ethiopia","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHarar_Dance.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Potawatomi_rain_dance.gif/500px-Potawatomi_rain_dance.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Potawatomi_rain_dance.gif/500px-Potawatomi_rain_dance.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rain dance, ca. 1920 (from the Potawatomi agency, presumably Prairie Band Potawatomi people)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APotawatomi_rain_dance.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/George_Catlin_-_Rainmaking_among_the_Mandan_-_1985.66.476_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg/500px-George_Catlin_-_Rainmaking_among_the_Mandan_-_1985.66.476_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/George_Catlin_-_Rainmaking_among_the_Mandan_-_1985.66.476_-_Smithsonian_American_Art_Museum.jpg/1280px-